MERGE cms.Post AS t
USING (SELECT N'30-years-of-pokemon-cards-a-collectors-timeline' AS Slug) AS s
ON t.Slug = s.Slug
WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET
    Title = N'30 Years of Pokémon Cards: A Collector''s Timeline',
    Excerpt = N'From the 1996 Japanese launch through the Wizards of the Coast years, Scarlet & Violet, and the Mega Evolution series, here is a collector-facing timeline of 30 years of Pokémon cards—plus where to go next at a local shop.',
    Body = N'<p>Last updated: October 2026</p>
<p>Thirty years is a long time for a piece of cardboard to stay in a kid’s pocket and a dealer’s case at the same time. The Pokémon Trading Card Game did both. It began in Japan in 1996, crossed into English-language shops under Wizards of the Coast, grew through later publisher eras, and in 2026 is living through a Mega Evolution series and a 30th Celebration set that put the anniversary on the pack itself.</p>
<p>This is not a price guide. NTCA will not tell you what a first-edition Base Set card “should” bring or which modern secret rare is due to spike. It is a map of eras, so a parent, a returning collector, or a shop owner can place a binder in time and know what to ask for at the counter.</p>
<h2>1996: the Japanese start</h2>
<p>The Pokémon Company’s own corporate history marks 1996 as the year the Pokémon Trading Card Game launched in Japan. That is the date that makes 2026 a 30th year. The original audience was the same mix we still see: children who wanted the creatures, and hobby players who wanted a game that fit on a table.</p>
<p>If you collect Japanese cards today, you are not collecting a footnote. You are collecting the first language of the game. Condition, edition marks, and set symbols still need a careful eye. A shop that handles Japanese Pokémon is worth a phone call before you mail a stack to a stranger.</p>
<h2>The Wizards of the Coast years</h2>
<p>English-speaking collectors usually meet Pokémon through the Wizards of the Coast era: the late-1990s explosion, schoolyard trades, and the first wave of cards that now sit in toploaders at every show. Wizards published the English-language game in those early years. The look is familiar even if you have not opened a pack since then—thick borders, a smaller set list than modern releases, and names people still use as shorthand (“Base,” “Jungle,” “the old stuff”).</p>
<p>We are not going to invent print runs or a single “this is when it got big” month. If you are returning, take a binder to a shop and ask them to sort WotC from later English cards. The backs, set symbols, and copyright lines are different enough that a good counter person can teach you in five minutes. That lesson is worth more than a forum argument about shadows and stamps.</p>
<h2>Later English eras, in collector shorthand</h2>
<p>After Wizards, The Pokémon Company International became the name on the box English collectors buy today. Between that handoff and the present, the game moved through several long series that shops still use as aisle labels: EX-era, Diamond &amp; Pearl and Platinum, the Black &amp; White and XY years, Sun &amp; Moon, Sword &amp; Shield. Each era changed finishes, set size, and how “a rare” looks.</p>
<p>You do not need to memorize every set code. You need to know which era a binder is from so you sleeve it correctly and so you do not mix a playable modern stack with a vintage stack you meant to keep still. When in doubt, keep eras in separate binders. Shops and show dealers can work faster, and you will not ding a 1990s card with a 2024 toploader habit.</p>
<h2>Scarlet &amp; Violet: the modern flood</h2>
<p>The Scarlet &amp; Violet series is the modern English era most new collectors actually opened. Official Pokémon product schedules list SV sets from the 2023 launch through 2025 specials such as Black Bolt and White Flare. The era taught a new generation special illustration rares, bigger set lists, and the feeling that a new release was always two months away.</p>
<p>If a child started in Pocket and then asked for “real cards,” there is a good chance the first cardboard they held was Scarlet &amp; Violet. That is fine. It is a living era with singles in almost every shop case. It is also a lot of bulk. Sort it. Sleeve the art you love. Do not assume every holo is a grading candidate.</p>
<h2>Mega Evolution, then an anniversary, then Delta Reign</h2>
<p>The current English series on shop walls is <strong>Mega Evolution</strong>. Official Pokémon press and product pages place the main sets on this calendar:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mega Evolution</strong> — September 26, 2025</li>
<li><strong>Phantasmal Flames</strong> — November 14, 2025</li>
<li><strong>Ascended Heroes</strong> — January 30, 2026</li>
<li><strong>Perfect Order</strong> — March 27, 2026</li>
<li><strong>Chaos Rising</strong> — May 22, 2026</li>
<li><strong>Pitch Black</strong> — July 17, 2026</li>
<li><strong>Delta Reign</strong> — November 6, 2026</li>
</ul>
<p>In the middle of that run, <strong>30th Celebration</strong> arrived on September 16, 2026. Official Pokémon pages file it with the Mega Evolution series and describe it as an anniversary expansion: a large card list, a Pikachu-in-every-pack collectible hook, and classic cards returning alongside new rarities. It is the set that makes the “30 years” conversation visible on a booster wrapper.</p>
<p>Delta Reign is the next regular expansion after that party. Official press (August 20, 2026) set street date at November 6, 2026 and placed Play! Pokémon prereleases at participating independent retailers beginning October 24, 2026. If you are reading this in late October, that window is now. Find a shop, do not camp a rumor.</p>
<p>Running alongside all of this is <strong>Pokémon TCG Pocket</strong>, the digital app that launched October 30, 2024. It is part of the 30-year story as an on-ramp, not as a replacement set. Digital cards stay digital.</p>
<h2>How to use a timeline in a shop or at a show</h2>
<p>A timeline is a sorting tool.</p>
<p>Parents can ask, “Do you have anything from the era my kid actually plays?” instead of “Do you have the expensive one from the video.” Collectors can keep WotC, later modern, Scarlet &amp; Violet, and Mega Evolution in different boxes so a show dealer can look without dumping a binder. Shop owners can label cases the same way customers search: vintage English, Japanese, SV, Mega Evolution, sealed.</p>
<p>If you want a physical next step this month, use the <a href="/shops">NTCA shop directory</a> for a store that carries the era you care about, check the <a href="/calendar">calendar</a> for a show with a Pokémon-heavy floor, and keep <a href="/new-to-trading-cards">New to Trading Cards</a> open when condition language starts flying. Thirty years of cards is a lot of joy. It fits better in a labeled box than in a myth about what you should have bought in 1999.</p>
<p>Pokémon is a trademark of Nintendo, Creatures, GAME FREAK, and The Pokémon Company. NTCA is not affiliated with Nintendo, Creatures, GAME FREAK, or The Pokémon Company.</p>',
    BodyMarkdown = N'Last updated: October 2026

Thirty years is a long time for a piece of cardboard to stay in a kid’s pocket and a dealer’s case at the same time. The Pokémon Trading Card Game did both. It began in Japan in 1996, crossed into English-language shops under Wizards of the Coast, grew through later publisher eras, and in 2026 is living through a Mega Evolution series and a 30th Celebration set that put the anniversary on the pack itself.

This is not a price guide. NTCA will not tell you what a first-edition Base Set card “should” bring or which modern secret rare is due to spike. It is a map of eras, so a parent, a returning collector, or a shop owner can place a binder in time and know what to ask for at the counter.

## 1996: the Japanese start

The Pokémon Company’s own corporate history marks 1996 as the year the Pokémon Trading Card Game launched in Japan. That is the date that makes 2026 a 30th year. The original audience was the same mix we still see: children who wanted the creatures, and hobby players who wanted a game that fit on a table.

If you collect Japanese cards today, you are not collecting a footnote. You are collecting the first language of the game. Condition, edition marks, and set symbols still need a careful eye. A shop that handles Japanese Pokémon is worth a phone call before you mail a stack to a stranger.

## The Wizards of the Coast years

English-speaking collectors usually meet Pokémon through the Wizards of the Coast era: the late-1990s explosion, schoolyard trades, and the first wave of cards that now sit in toploaders at every show. Wizards published the English-language game in those early years. The look is familiar even if you have not opened a pack since then—thick borders, a smaller set list than modern releases, and names people still use as shorthand (“Base,” “Jungle,” “the old stuff”).

We are not going to invent print runs or a single “this is when it got big” month. If you are returning, take a binder to a shop and ask them to sort WotC from later English cards. The backs, set symbols, and copyright lines are different enough that a good counter person can teach you in five minutes. That lesson is worth more than a forum argument about shadows and stamps.

## Later English eras, in collector shorthand

After Wizards, The Pokémon Company International became the name on the box English collectors buy today. Between that handoff and the present, the game moved through several long series that shops still use as aisle labels: EX-era, Diamond & Pearl and Platinum, the Black & White and XY years, Sun & Moon, Sword & Shield. Each era changed finishes, set size, and how “a rare” looks.

You do not need to memorize every set code. You need to know which era a binder is from so you sleeve it correctly and so you do not mix a playable modern stack with a vintage stack you meant to keep still. When in doubt, keep eras in separate binders. Shops and show dealers can work faster, and you will not ding a 1990s card with a 2024 toploader habit.

## Scarlet & Violet: the modern flood

The Scarlet & Violet series is the modern English era most new collectors actually opened. Official Pokémon product schedules list SV sets from the 2023 launch through 2025 specials such as Black Bolt and White Flare. The era taught a new generation special illustration rares, bigger set lists, and the feeling that a new release was always two months away.

If a child started in Pocket and then asked for “real cards,” there is a good chance the first cardboard they held was Scarlet & Violet. That is fine. It is a living era with singles in almost every shop case. It is also a lot of bulk. Sort it. Sleeve the art you love. Do not assume every holo is a grading candidate.

## Mega Evolution, then an anniversary, then Delta Reign

The current English series on shop walls is **Mega Evolution**. Official Pokémon press and product pages place the main sets on this calendar:

- **Mega Evolution** — September 26, 2025
- **Phantasmal Flames** — November 14, 2025
- **Ascended Heroes** — January 30, 2026
- **Perfect Order** — March 27, 2026
- **Chaos Rising** — May 22, 2026
- **Pitch Black** — July 17, 2026
- **Delta Reign** — November 6, 2026

In the middle of that run, **30th Celebration** arrived on September 16, 2026. Official Pokémon pages file it with the Mega Evolution series and describe it as an anniversary expansion: a large card list, a Pikachu-in-every-pack collectible hook, and classic cards returning alongside new rarities. It is the set that makes the “30 years” conversation visible on a booster wrapper.

Delta Reign is the next regular expansion after that party. Official press (August 20, 2026) set street date at November 6, 2026 and placed Play! Pokémon prereleases at participating independent retailers beginning October 24, 2026. If you are reading this in late October, that window is now. Find a shop, do not camp a rumor.

Running alongside all of this is **Pokémon TCG Pocket**, the digital app that launched October 30, 2024. It is part of the 30-year story as an on-ramp, not as a replacement set. Digital cards stay digital.

## How to use a timeline in a shop or at a show

A timeline is a sorting tool.

Parents can ask, “Do you have anything from the era my kid actually plays?” instead of “Do you have the expensive one from the video.” Collectors can keep WotC, later modern, Scarlet & Violet, and Mega Evolution in different boxes so a show dealer can look without dumping a binder. Shop owners can label cases the same way customers search: vintage English, Japanese, SV, Mega Evolution, sealed.

If you want a physical next step this month, use the [NTCA shop directory](/shops) for a store that carries the era you care about, check the [calendar](/calendar) for a show with a Pokémon-heavy floor, and keep [New to Trading Cards](/new-to-trading-cards) open when condition language starts flying. Thirty years of cards is a lot of joy. It fits better in a labeled box than in a myth about what you should have bought in 1999.

Pokémon is a trademark of Nintendo, Creatures, GAME FREAK, and The Pokémon Company. NTCA is not affiliated with Nintendo, Creatures, GAME FREAK, or The Pokémon Company.
