WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET
    Title = N'Protecting and Grading Pokémon Cards: Sleeves to Slabs',
    Excerpt = N'Most Pokémon cards need a sleeve, not a slab. Here is a practical NTCA path from penny sleeves and toploaders to binders and storage—and how to decide when PSA, BGS, or CGC is actually worth it.',
    Body = N'<p>The fastest way to damage a Pokémon card is to love it with bare hands and a backpack. The second-fastest is to mail it to a grading company before you have learned what “good condition” looks like. Protection is a ladder. Most cards should stop on an early rung. A few belong in a slab.</p>
<p>This is the path we walk parents and new collectors through at shops and shows. We will name the common supplies and the three graders you will hear at every table—PSA, BGS, and CGC. We will not quote submission fees, service levels, or “what a 10 is worth.” Those numbers move. The habits do not.</p>
<h2>Start with the card in your hand</h2>
<p>Look at it under a shop light or a window, not a phone flash. Check corners, edges, the face, and the back. A little whitening on a played uncommon is normal. A crease through a chase holo is a different conversation. If you are still learning the words, <a href="/new-to-trading-cards">New to Trading Cards</a> covers condition language. The short version: mint is rare, “I just opened it” is not the same as perfect, and centering (how even the border is) matters to graders more than it matters to a kid building a deck.</p>
<p>Do this inspection *before* you sleeve, so you are not grading a fingerprint you are about to add.</p>
<h2>Penny sleeves, then a hard layer</h2>
<p>A <strong>penny sleeve</strong> is a thin, soft plastic envelope. It is the cheapest insurance in the hobby. Slide the card in so the open end is at the top. Do this at the table when you open a pack—not in the car, not after the card has ridden loose in a box.</p>
<p>A sleeve alone is not enough for a card you care about. Add a <strong>toploader</strong> (a rigid plastic holder) or a <strong>semi-rigid</strong> holder for cards that will travel, trade, or sit in a box. The sleeve goes on first. The toploader is the armor. Forcing a sleeved card into a toploader that is too tight will wipe the edges. If it fights you, stop and ask the counter for a size that matches modern Pokémon card stock.</p>
<p><strong>Team bags</strong> or resealable outer bags keep dust off the toploader—optional at home, wise at a show. Skip magnetic one-touch holders until you know how to seat a card without pinching a corner. They look premium. They punish haste.</p>
<h2>Binders and pages for the cards you will actually look at</h2>
<p>A binder is for cards you want to see, not for every card you own. Use pages designed for standard-size trading cards. Side-loading pockets are kinder than top-loading pages that dump a card when you tilt the binder. Do not shove a toploader into a 9-pocket page; that is how pages split and corners ding.</p>
<p>Two binder habits that prevent regret:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>One card per pocket</strong>, sleeved. Doubling up looks efficient and scratches both cards.</li>
<li><strong>Do not overfill.</strong> A stuffed binder is a taco waiting to happen in a backpack.</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep a second, cheaper binder or a cardboard storage box for bulk commons. The pretty binder is the one you show people. The bulk box is how you keep the pretty binder from becoming a warehouse.</p>
<h2>Storage that survives a closet</h2>
<p>Cards want the same climate you do: indoor temperature, low humidity, no attic, no car trunk, no damp basement. Use a closet shelf or a closet floor that does not flood. Keep boxes off concrete. Avoid sunlight on a display that faces a window—holo film and ink both fade.</p>
<p>A <strong>deck box</strong> is for a playable deck you will shuffle. A <strong>storage box</strong> is for sorted bulk. A <strong>display case</strong> is for a handful of favorites. Do not store a collection long-term in the ETB it came in if that box will be stacked or kicked.</p>
<p>At shows, keep one trade binder in hand, toploaders in a zip pouch, and slabs in a case that zips. The rest stays home. Crowded aisles are where “I just set it down for a second” becomes a story you tell for years.</p>
<h2>When grading is worth the wait</h2>
<p>Grading is a third-party opinion in a plastic case. PSA, BGS, and CGC are the names you will hear most in North American Pokémon. Each has its own labels, population reports, and collector following. Shops and show dealers will have preferences. None of them can make a creased card new.</p>
<p>Grade when most of these are true:</p>
<ul>
<li>The card is one you would be sad to shuffle or to keep in a binder that travels.</li>
<li>You have already looked at it the way a grader will: surface, corners, edges, centering.</li>
<li>You can leave the card in a queue for a while. Grading is not overnight retail.</li>
<li>You have a reason beyond “the internet said to slab everything.” Reasons that count: long-term display, insurance documentation, selling a true high-end piece through a dealer who wants a holder, or you simply love that card enough to lock it.</li>
</ul>
<p>Skip grading when:</p>
<ul>
<li>You still play the card, or a child still wants to hold it.</li>
<li>The card is modern bulk you opened last week. Volume is not a grading strategy.</li>
<li>You have not priced the *total* cost—fees, shipping, insurance, and the risk of a grade you will not like. Look up current fees on the grader’s own site. We will not invent them.</li>
<li>Someone is pressuring you to slab so they can flip it. That is their business plan, not your collection.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you do submit, ask a shop that handles submissions how they pack cards, follow the grader’s current rules, and photograph what you send. Shop owners: a five-minute “should I grade this?” look is high-trust work. Be willing to say no.</p>
<h2>A simple home setup</h2>
<p>Buy a pack of penny sleeves, a small stack of toploaders, one side-loading binder, and one cardboard storage box. That kit covers a first year of Pokémon for most families. Add a slab only when a specific card earns it. Use the <a href="/shops">shop directory</a> to buy supplies where someone will show you how to seat a sleeve, and check the <a href="/calendar">calendar</a> before you carry slabs into a show without a case.</p>
<p>Protect first. Grade rarely. Enjoy the cards in between.</p>
<p>Pokémon is a trademark of Nintendo, Creatures, GAME FREAK, and The Pokémon Company. NTCA is not affiliated with Nintendo, Creatures, GAME FREAK, or The Pokémon Company.</p>',
    BodyMarkdown = N'The fastest way to damage a Pokémon card is to love it with bare hands and a backpack. The second-fastest is to mail it to a grading company before you have learned what “good condition” looks like. Protection is a ladder. Most cards should stop on an early rung. A few belong in a slab.

This is the path we walk parents and new collectors through at shops and shows. We will name the common supplies and the three graders you will hear at every table—PSA, BGS, and CGC. We will not quote submission fees, service levels, or “what a 10 is worth.” Those numbers move. The habits do not.

## Start with the card in your hand

Look at it under a shop light or a window, not a phone flash. Check corners, edges, the face, and the back. A little whitening on a played uncommon is normal. A crease through a chase holo is a different conversation. If you are still learning the words, [New to Trading Cards](/new-to-trading-cards) covers condition language. The short version: mint is rare, “I just opened it” is not the same as perfect, and centering (how even the border is) matters to graders more than it matters to a kid building a deck.

Do this inspection *before* you sleeve, so you are not grading a fingerprint you are about to add.

## Penny sleeves, then a hard layer

A **penny sleeve** is a thin, soft plastic envelope. It is the cheapest insurance in the hobby. Slide the card in so the open end is at the top. Do this at the table when you open a pack—not in the car, not after the card has ridden loose in a box.

A sleeve alone is not enough for a card you care about. Add a **toploader** (a rigid plastic holder) or a **semi-rigid** holder for cards that will travel, trade, or sit in a box. The sleeve goes on first. The toploader is the armor. Forcing a sleeved card into a toploader that is too tight will wipe the edges. If it fights you, stop and ask the counter for a size that matches modern Pokémon card stock.

**Team bags** or resealable outer bags keep dust off the toploader—optional at home, wise at a show. Skip magnetic one-touch holders until you know how to seat a card without pinching a corner. They look premium. They punish haste.

## Binders and pages for the cards you will actually look at

A binder is for cards you want to see, not for every card you own. Use pages designed for standard-size trading cards. Side-loading pockets are kinder than top-loading pages that dump a card when you tilt the binder. Do not shove a toploader into a 9-pocket page; that is how pages split and corners ding.

Two binder habits that prevent regret:

- **One card per pocket**, sleeved. Doubling up looks efficient and scratches both cards.
- **Do not overfill.** A stuffed binder is a taco waiting to happen in a backpack.

Keep a second, cheaper binder or a cardboard storage box for bulk commons. The pretty binder is the one you show people. The bulk box is how you keep the pretty binder from becoming a warehouse.

## Storage that survives a closet

Cards want the same climate you do: indoor temperature, low humidity, no attic, no car trunk, no damp basement. Use a closet shelf or a closet floor that does not flood. Keep boxes off concrete. Avoid sunlight on a display that faces a window—holo film and ink both fade.

A **deck box** is for a playable deck you will shuffle. A **storage box** is for sorted bulk. A **display case** is for a handful of favorites. Do not store a collection long-term in the ETB it came in if that box will be stacked or kicked.

At shows, keep one trade binder in hand, toploaders in a zip pouch, and slabs in a case that zips. The rest stays home. Crowded aisles are where “I just set it down for a second” becomes a story you tell for years.

## When grading is worth the wait

Grading is a third-party opinion in a plastic case. PSA, BGS, and CGC are the names you will hear most in North American Pokémon. Each has its own labels, population reports, and collector following. Shops and show dealers will have preferences. None of them can make a creased card new.

Grade when most of these are true:

- The card is one you would be sad to shuffle or to keep in a binder that travels.
- You have already looked at it the way a grader will: surface, corners, edges, centering.
- You can leave the card in a queue for a while. Grading is not overnight retail.
- You have a reason beyond “the internet said to slab everything.” Reasons that count: long-term display, insurance documentation, selling a true high-end piece through a dealer who wants a holder, or you simply love that card enough to lock it.

Skip grading when:

- You still play the card, or a child still wants to hold it.
- The card is modern bulk you opened last week. Volume is not a grading strategy.
- You have not priced the *total* cost—fees, shipping, insurance, and the risk of a grade you will not like. Look up current fees on the grader’s own site. We will not invent them.
- Someone is pressuring you to slab so they can flip it. That is their business plan, not your collection.

If you do submit, ask a shop that handles submissions how they pack cards, follow the grader’s current rules, and photograph what you send. Shop owners: a five-minute “should I grade this?” look is high-trust work. Be willing to say no.

## A simple home setup

Buy a pack of penny sleeves, a small stack of toploaders, one side-loading binder, and one cardboard storage box. That kit covers a first year of Pokémon for most families. Add a slab only when a specific card earns it. Use the [shop directory](/shops) to buy supplies where someone will show you how to seat a sleeve, and check the [calendar](/calendar) before you carry slabs into a show without a case.

Protect first. Grade rarely. Enjoy the cards in between.

