WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET
    Title = N'What Is Pokémon TCG Pocket? A Parent''s Guide',
    Excerpt = N'Pokémon TCG Pocket is a free-to-start phone app for collecting digital Pokémon cards. Here is a practical NTCA guide for parents on daily packs, in-app purchases, trading, and screen-time habits.',
    Body = N'<p>If your kid suddenly knows every Pokémon name on the playground again, there is a good chance Pokémon TCG Pocket is on their phone. The app is a digital card-collecting game: swipe, open a pack, and fill a binder you can carry in a pocket. It is not the same thing as the cardboard Pokémon Trading Card Game you buy at a hobby shop, and that distinction matters for families.</p>
<p>This guide is for parents, new collectors, and shop owners who keep getting the same question at the counter: “Is Pocket safe, is it free, and will it replace real cards?” Short answers: it is free to start, it is not free of spending pressure, and it works best as a companion to the hobby—not a substitute for a local shop.</p>
<h2>What Pocket actually is</h2>
<p>Pokémon Trading Card Game Pocket launched on October 30, 2024 as a free-to-start iOS and Android app. It was co-developed by The Pokémon Company, Creatures Inc., and DeNA. The pitch is simple: collect digital Pokémon cards in short sessions, enjoy pack-opening animations that only a screen can do, and build decks for casual battles.</p>
<p>DeNA’s launch announcement described basic gameplay as free, with some items available for purchase, and said players can open two booster packs every day at no cost. That daily pair of packs is the habit that keeps kids opening the app. By December 12, 2024, DeNA and The Pokémon Company reported more than 60 million downloads since launch. Those figures are official milestones, not a reason to treat the app as homework. Pocket cards live in an account. They are not the same object as a physical card in a penny sleeve.</p>
<h2>Free to start is not the same as free forever</h2>
<p>The honest parent framing is “free to start.” Your child can download the app, open the daily free packs, and play without paying. The app also sells extras. Official materials do not publish a single permanent price list in a way that stays current, and NTCA will not invent in-app prices here. Open the store screen in the app itself and read what a purchase actually buys before anyone taps Confirm.</p>
<p>What parents should watch is not one pack—it is the rhythm:</p>
<ul>
<li>Daily free packs create a streak. Missing a day feels like leaving money on the table, even when no money is involved.</li>
<li>Limited-time cosmetics, extra packs, and convenience items show up around new digital expansions. Kids feel that clock.</li>
<li>A saved payment method on a shared tablet or a parent’s phone is an accident waiting to happen.</li>
</ul>
<p>Set the store and payment rules before the first weekend, not after a surprise email receipt.</p>
<h2>Daily packs, trading, and what you should not assume</h2>
<p>Two free packs a day is the official, documented habit. Everything else about pack contents—how often a rare card appears, what a “hit” looks like in a given set—belongs in the app’s current help text and official Pokémon channels. Odds change. Do not trust a screenshot from a classmate.</p>
<p>Trading exists in Pocket, and kids will talk about it the way they talk about playground swaps. The rules, friend limits, waiting periods, and what can or cannot be traded are set by the developer and can change with updates. Check the in-app trading screens and official Pocket support before you tell a child “yes, you can trade that.”</p>
<p>One rule we will state clearly, because families mix this up: a Pocket card is not a physical card. You cannot sell it to a dealer, send it to PSA, BGS, or CGC, or redeem it for cardboard at a shop. If someone online offers to “cash out” a Pocket binder, treat that as a warning, not a feature.</p>
<h2>Screen-time and spending tips that actually work</h2>
<p>Hobby shops see the parent version of this every week. A child loves the art. A parent wants a limit that does not turn into a fight. Try a plan you can say out loud:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Name the device and the account.</strong> Pocket should live on a device you can pick up. The Apple or Google account that pays for it should be yours, with purchase approval turned on.</li>
<li><strong>Decide the daily window.</strong> Two free packs do not require an hour. A 15-minute window after homework is plenty for opening, organizing, and a battle. If the app is still open when the timer ends, it closes.</li>
<li><strong>Separate “free fun” from “paid fun.”</strong> Daily packs are the baseline. Extra purchases are a birthday, a completed chore chart, or a no. Do not negotiate pack prices at bedtime.</li>
<li><strong>Talk about luck before the first paid pack.</strong> Packs are random. Official sources publish odds in the places they choose to publish them; we will not invent percentages. The family rule is the same as a physical booster: you are paying for the experience of opening, not for a promised card.</li>
<li><strong>Watch the social layer.</strong> Friend requests, trade offers, and chat-adjacent features need the same rules you use for any game with strangers. If you would not let a child mail a card to an unknown adult, do not let them trade digital cards with one either.</li>
<li><strong>Keep a cardboard off-ramp.</strong> When a kid is ready to hold a real card, walk into a shop together. That visit teaches condition, sleeves, and why a local counter is different from an app store.</li>
</ol>
<p>If you run a shop, this is a gift. Parents who understand Pocket are easier to help when they ask for a starter deck or a prerelease seat. You are not competing with a free app. You are the place that turns a swipe into a hobby.</p>
<h2>A calm way to say yes</h2>
<p>You do not have to ban Pocket to be a careful parent. You can say: two free packs, our payment settings, a time limit, and we will visit a real shop this month so you can see what a card feels like.</p>
<p>NTCA exists to keep the in-person hobby easy to find. Use the <a href="/shops">shop directory</a> to locate an independent store, read <a href="/new-to-trading-cards">New to Trading Cards</a> for glossary and condition basics, and check the <a href="/calendar">calendar</a> when you want a show instead of a sofa. Pocket can live on the phone. The community still lives at the counter.</p>
<p>Pokémon is a trademark of Nintendo, Creatures, GAME FREAK, and The Pokémon Company. NTCA is not affiliated with Nintendo, Creatures, GAME FREAK, or The Pokémon Company.</p>',
    BodyMarkdown = N'If your kid suddenly knows every Pokémon name on the playground again, there is a good chance Pokémon TCG Pocket is on their phone. The app is a digital card-collecting game: swipe, open a pack, and fill a binder you can carry in a pocket. It is not the same thing as the cardboard Pokémon Trading Card Game you buy at a hobby shop, and that distinction matters for families.

This guide is for parents, new collectors, and shop owners who keep getting the same question at the counter: “Is Pocket safe, is it free, and will it replace real cards?” Short answers: it is free to start, it is not free of spending pressure, and it works best as a companion to the hobby—not a substitute for a local shop.

## What Pocket actually is

Pokémon Trading Card Game Pocket launched on October 30, 2024 as a free-to-start iOS and Android app. It was co-developed by The Pokémon Company, Creatures Inc., and DeNA. The pitch is simple: collect digital Pokémon cards in short sessions, enjoy pack-opening animations that only a screen can do, and build decks for casual battles.

DeNA’s launch announcement described basic gameplay as free, with some items available for purchase, and said players can open two booster packs every day at no cost. That daily pair of packs is the habit that keeps kids opening the app. By December 12, 2024, DeNA and The Pokémon Company reported more than 60 million downloads since launch. Those figures are official milestones, not a reason to treat the app as homework. Pocket cards live in an account. They are not the same object as a physical card in a penny sleeve.

## Free to start is not the same as free forever

The honest parent framing is “free to start.” Your child can download the app, open the daily free packs, and play without paying. The app also sells extras. Official materials do not publish a single permanent price list in a way that stays current, and NTCA will not invent in-app prices here. Open the store screen in the app itself and read what a purchase actually buys before anyone taps Confirm.

What parents should watch is not one pack—it is the rhythm:

- Daily free packs create a streak. Missing a day feels like leaving money on the table, even when no money is involved.
- Limited-time cosmetics, extra packs, and convenience items show up around new digital expansions. Kids feel that clock.
- A saved payment method on a shared tablet or a parent’s phone is an accident waiting to happen.

Set the store and payment rules before the first weekend, not after a surprise email receipt.

## Daily packs, trading, and what you should not assume

Two free packs a day is the official, documented habit. Everything else about pack contents—how often a rare card appears, what a “hit” looks like in a given set—belongs in the app’s current help text and official Pokémon channels. Odds change. Do not trust a screenshot from a classmate.

Trading exists in Pocket, and kids will talk about it the way they talk about playground swaps. The rules, friend limits, waiting periods, and what can or cannot be traded are set by the developer and can change with updates. Check the in-app trading screens and official Pocket support before you tell a child “yes, you can trade that.”

One rule we will state clearly, because families mix this up: a Pocket card is not a physical card. You cannot sell it to a dealer, send it to PSA, BGS, or CGC, or redeem it for cardboard at a shop. If someone online offers to “cash out” a Pocket binder, treat that as a warning, not a feature.

## Screen-time and spending tips that actually work

Hobby shops see the parent version of this every week. A child loves the art. A parent wants a limit that does not turn into a fight. Try a plan you can say out loud:

1. **Name the device and the account.** Pocket should live on a device you can pick up. The Apple or Google account that pays for it should be yours, with purchase approval turned on.
2. **Decide the daily window.** Two free packs do not require an hour. A 15-minute window after homework is plenty for opening, organizing, and a battle. If the app is still open when the timer ends, it closes.
3. **Separate “free fun” from “paid fun.”** Daily packs are the baseline. Extra purchases are a birthday, a completed chore chart, or a no. Do not negotiate pack prices at bedtime.
4. **Talk about luck before the first paid pack.** Packs are random. Official sources publish odds in the places they choose to publish them; we will not invent percentages. The family rule is the same as a physical booster: you are paying for the experience of opening, not for a promised card.
5. **Watch the social layer.** Friend requests, trade offers, and chat-adjacent features need the same rules you use for any game with strangers. If you would not let a child mail a card to an unknown adult, do not let them trade digital cards with one either.
6. **Keep a cardboard off-ramp.** When a kid is ready to hold a real card, walk into a shop together. That visit teaches condition, sleeves, and why a local counter is different from an app store.

If you run a shop, this is a gift. Parents who understand Pocket are easier to help when they ask for a starter deck or a prerelease seat. You are not competing with a free app. You are the place that turns a swipe into a hobby.

## A calm way to say yes

You do not have to ban Pocket to be a careful parent. You can say: two free packs, our payment settings, a time limit, and we will visit a real shop this month so you can see what a card feels like.

NTCA exists to keep the in-person hobby easy to find. Use the [shop directory](/shops) to locate an independent store, read [New to Trading Cards](/new-to-trading-cards) for glossary and condition basics, and check the [calendar](/calendar) when you want a show instead of a sofa. Pocket can live on the phone. The community still lives at the counter.

Pokémon is a trademark of Nintendo, Creatures, GAME FREAK, and The Pokémon Company. NTCA is not affiliated with Nintendo, Creatures, GAME FREAK, or The Pokémon Company.
