<p>Hobby shops see the parent version of this every week. A child loves the art. A parent wants a limit that does not turn into a fight. Try a plan you can say out loud:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Name the device and the account.</strong> Pocket should live on a device you can pick up. The Apple or Google account that pays for it should be yours, with purchase approval turned on.</li>
<li><strong>Decide the daily window.</strong> Two free packs do not require an hour. A 15-minute window after homework is plenty for opening, organizing, and a battle. If the app is still open when the timer ends, it closes.</li>
<li><strong>Separate “free fun” from “paid fun.”</strong> Daily packs are the baseline. Extra purchases are a birthday, a completed chore chart, or a no. Do not negotiate pack prices at bedtime.</li>
<li><strong>Talk about luck before the first paid pack.</strong> Packs are random. Official sources publish odds in the places they choose to publish them; we will not invent percentages. The family rule is the same as a physical booster: you are paying for the experience of opening, not for a promised card.</li>
<li><strong>Watch the social layer.</strong> Friend requests, trade offers, and chat-adjacent features need the same rules you use for any game with strangers. If you would not let a child mail a card to an unknown adult, do not let them trade digital cards with one either.</li>
<li><strong>Keep a cardboard off-ramp.</strong> When a kid is ready to hold a real card, walk into a shop together. That visit teaches condition, sleeves, and why a local counter is different from an app store.</li>
</ol>
<p>If you run a shop, this is a gift. Parents who understand Pocket are easier to help when they ask for a starter deck or a prerelease seat. You are not competing with a free app. You are the place that turns a swipe into a hobby.</p>
<h2>A calm way to say yes</h2>
<p>You do not have to ban Pocket to be a careful parent. You can say: two free packs, our payment settings, a time limit, and we will visit a real shop this month so you can see what a card feels like.</p>
<p>NTCA exists to keep the in-person hobby easy to find. Use the <a href="/shops">shop directory</a> to locate an independent store, read <a href="/new-to-trading-cards">New to Trading Cards</a> for glossary and condition basics, and check the <a href="/calendar">calendar</a> when you want a show instead of a sofa. Pocket can live on the phone. The community still lives at the counter.</p>
